Usage: sort [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Sort lines of text files.
With no FILE, or when FILE is -, read standard input.

OPTIONS:
  -b, --ignore-leading-blanks    ignore leading blanks
  -d, --dictionary-order         consider only blanks and alphanumeric
                                 characters
  -f, --ignore-case              fold lower case to upper case
  -g, --general-numeric-sort     compare according to string numerical value
  -i, --ignore-nonprinting       consider only printable characters
  -h, --human-numeric-sort       compare human readable numbers (e.g., 1K, 2M)
  -M, --month-sort               compare as month names
  -m, --merge                    merge already sorted files
  -n, --numeric-sort             compare according to string numerical value
  -V, --version-sort             compare version numbers naturally
  -R, --random-sort              sort by random hash of keys
      --random-source ARG        get random bytes from FILE
  -r, --reverse                  reverse the result of comparisons
  -S, --buffer-size ARG          use SIZE for the main memory buffer; accepted
                                 as a memory hint
      --parallel ARG             change the number of sorts run concurrently to
                                 N; accepted as a concurrency hint
  -s, --stable                   stabilize sort by disabling last-resort
                                 comparison
  -u, --unique                   output only the first of equal runs
  -z, --zero-terminated          line delimiter is NUL, not newline
      --files0-from ARG          read input from the file specified
  -c                             check whether input is sorted; diagnose-first
      --check[=ARG]              check whether input is sorted; accepts
                                 =diagnose-first|quiet|silent
  -C, --check-silent             check whether input is sorted quietly
      --debug                    print sort key diagnostics to standard error
  -o, --output ARG               write result to FILE instead of standard output
      --files0-from ARG          read input from the files specified by
                                 NUL-terminated names in FILE
      --batch-size ARG           merge at most NMERGE inputs at once; accepted
                                 as a merge hint
      --compress-program ARG     compress temporaries with PROG; accepted as a
                                 compression hint
  -T, --temporary-directory ARG  use DIR for temporaries; accepted as a
                                 temporary-directory hint
      --sort ARG                 set sort order; 'version' enables version sort
  -t, --field-separator ARG      use SEP instead of non-blank to blank
                                 transition
  -k, --key ARG                  sort via a key; KEYDEF has form F[.C][,F[.C]]
      --help                     display this help and exit
      --version                  output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
